istent penalizing hashing for cross-modal retrieval . IEEE Transactions on Multimedia 27 , pp. 4613–4626 . Cited by: Introduction . Qwen Team (2026a) Qwen3. 5-omni technical report . arXiv preprint arXiv:2604.15804 . Cited by: Appendix B , Table 2 , Table 3 . Qwen Team (2026b) Qwen3.6-27B: flagship-level coding in a 27B dense model . External Links: Link Cited by: Appendix B , QA-based Caption Evaluation. . Y. Rong, J. Wang, G. Lei, S. Yang, and L. Liu (2025) Audiogenie: a training-free multi-agent framework for diverse multimodality-to-multiaudio generation . In Proceedings of the 33rd ACM International Conference on Multimedia , pp. 8872–8881 . Cited by: Introduction . S. Sakshi, U. Tyagi, S. Kumar, A. Seth, R. Selvakumar, O. Nieto, R. Duraiswami, S. Ghosh, and D. Manocha (2025) Mmau: a massive multi-task audio understanding and reasoning benchmark . In International Conference on Learning Representations , Vol. 2025 , pp. 84929–84964 . Cited by: QA-based Caption Evaluation. . Z. Shao, Y. Luo, C. Lu, Z. Ren, J. Hu, T. Ye, Z. Gou, S. Ma, and X. Zhang (2025) Deepseekmath-v2: towards self-verifiable mathematical reasoning . arXiv preprint arXiv:2511.22570 . Cited by: Introduction , Reinforcement Learning for MLLMs , Group Relative Policy Optimization. . B. Wu, C. Yan, C. Hu, C. Yi, C. Feng, F. Tian, F. Shen, G. Yu, H. Zhang, J. Li, et al. (2025) Step-audio 2 technical report . arXiv preprint arXiv:2507.16632 . Cited by: Appendix B , Table 2 . J. Xu, Z. Guo, J. He, H. Hu, T. He, S. Bai, K. Chen, J. Wang, Y. Fan, K. Dang, B. Zhang, X. Wang, Y. Chu, and J. Lin (2025a) Qwen2.5-Omni technical report . External Links: 2503.20215 , Link Cited by: Appendix B , Appendix B , Supervised Fine-Tuning. , Table 2 , Table 2 , Table 3 , Table 3 . J. Xu, Z. Guo, H. Hu, Y. Chu, X. Wang, J. He, Y. Wang, X. Shi, T. He, X. Zhu, et al. (2025b) Qwen3-omni technical report . arXiv preprint arXiv:2509.17765 . Cited by: Appendix B , Appendix B , Table 1 , Introduction , Audio Captioning , Table 2 , Table 3 . L. Yao, Y. Wei, Y. Zhang, L. Li, X. Chen, F. Song, Z. Wang, K. Ouyang, Y. Liu, L. Kong, et al. (2026) TimeChat-captioner: scripting multi-scene videos with time-aware and structural audio-visual captions . In Forty-third International Conference on Machine Learning , Cited by: Appendix B , Table 2 , Table 3 . Appendix Overview This appendix is organized into the following two parts: • A. AudioMapCap-44K: Dataset construction, summary statistics, and quality-control procedure. • B. Experimental Setup: Implementation details, training configurations, and evaluated baselines. Appendix A A. AudioMapCap-44K AudioMapCap-44K Statistics and Quality Control After detailed annotation and quality screening, the final AudioMapCap-44K dataset contains 43,870 caption pairs spanning 769.70 hours. Clip durations range from 5.132 to 180.256 seconds, with a mean of 63.162 seconds and a median of 56.401 seconds. Table 7 reports the audio-type and lyric distributions. Table 8 reports the duration distribution. Caption annotation. We select a subset of ASID-1M (Li et al. 2026 ) and use Gemini-3.1-Pro (Google DeepMind 2026 ) to annotate each selected clip through an iterative three-stage pipeline. First, the model generates an initial caption that describes acoustic events, temporal boundaries, and event-level attributes such as timbre, emotion, and background noise. Second, the pipeline examines the initial caption for missing or underspecified information and conducts multiple rounds of targeted questioning. The model answers each question using the source audio and video as evidence. The visual stream is used at this stage as auxiliary cross-modal evidence to verify or disambiguate acoustic events that are difficult to resolve from audio alone. Third, the pipeline merges the initial caption with the information collected from these multi-turn responses to produce a cohesive, time-aware dense caption. Table 7: Audio-type composition of AudioMapCap-44K. Marginal and exclusive shares use all 43,870 clips as the denominator. Lyric shares use the 31,442 clips containing music as the denominator. Category Count Share (%) Marginal audio types Speech 35,773 81.5 Sound effects 31,791 72.5 Music 31,442 71.7 Music with lyrics 8,721 19.9 Mutually exclusive combinations Speech only 2,357 5.4 Sound effects only 825 1.9 Music only 4,657 10.6 Speech + sound effects 9,246 21.1 Speech + music 5,065 11.5 Sound effects + music 2,615 6.0 Speech + sound effects + music 19,105 43.5 Lyrics among music-containing clips With lyrics 8,721 27.7 Without lyrics 22,721 72.3 Table 8: Clip-duration distribution of AudioMapCap-44K in 15-second intervals. Shares use all 43,870 clips as the denominator. Duration (s) Count Share (%) Duration (s) Count Share (%) 0–15 4,826 11.0 105–120 4,326 9.9 15–30 8,951 20.4 120–135 1,459 3.3 30–45 3,810 8.7 135–150 1,241 2.8 45–60 9,207 21.0 150–165 1,163 2.7 60–75 2,192 5.0 165–180 1,104 2.5 75–90 2,473 5.6 &gt; 180 &gt;180 24 0.1 90–105 3,094 7.1 – – – Quality screening. After caption construction, we apply two automatic checks to the full candidate set. First, rules remove captions that are too short, too long, or highly repetitive. Second, GPT-4.1 (Achiam et al. 2023 ) evaluates the completeness of each remaining caption and assigns a score from 1 to 5. We retain samples scoring 4 or 5. We then manually inspect a subset of the retained samples against the source audio for event coverage, temporal accuracy, and hallucinated events. This process combines full-set automatic screening with targeted human review, providing scalable quality control for constructing complete and time-aligned SFT pairs. Appendix B B. Experimental Setup Implementation Details Training setup. We initialize AudioMap from Qwen2.5-Omni (Xu et al. 2025a ) and train it on eight NVIDIA A800-SXM4 GPUs with 80 GB of memory each. We use a single model and training pipeline for both input settings rather than training separate audio-only and audio-visual variants. Throughout SFT and GRPO, the vision encoder remains frozen, while the modality aligner, LLM backbone, and language modeling head are trainable. SFT uses all 43,870 examples in AudioMapCap-44K, with 80% audio-only inputs and 20% paired audio-visual inputs. GRPO uses 12,500 examples randomly sampled from AudioMapCap-44K and takes audio-only inputs. We train each stage of both the SFT and GRPO curricula for one epoch. Supervised fine-tuning. For short clips, the learning rates are 1 × 10 − 5 1\\times 10^{-5} for the backbone, 5 × 10 − 5 5\\times 10^{-5} for the language modeling head, and 1 × 10 − 4 1\\times 10^{-4} for the aligner. We use a higher learning rate for the language modeling head than for the backbone because this head directly produces token predictions, allowing it to adapt more quickly to the structured dense-caption format. For long clips, we reduce the learning rates to 5 × 10 − 6 5\\times 10^{-6} , 2 × 10 − 5 2\\times 10^{-5} , and 2 × 10 − 5 2\\times 10^{-5} , respectively. Two-stage GRPO. The 12,500 GRPO examples are divided into two curriculum stages, as summarized in Table 9 . Stage 1 uses 6,000 clips shorter than 45 seconds and is approximately balanced across single and mixed audio types. It optimizes the policy with ESR and length regularization. Stage 2 uses 6,500 clips, including a small set shorter than 30 seconds, a majority between 30 and 90 seconds, and some longer than 90 seconds. It jointly optimizes the policy with ESR, ECTR, and length regularization. Table 9: Configuration of the two-stage GRPO curriculum. Reward weights are ordered as ( λ ESR , λ ECTR , λ len ) (\\lambda_{\\mathrm{ESR}},\\lambda_{\\mathrm{ECTR}},\\lambda_{\\mathrm{len}}) . Setting Stage 1 Stage 2 Training examples 6,000 6,500 Clip duration &lt; 45 &lt;45 s Mostly 30–90 s Reward weights ( 1.0 , 0 , 0.2 ) (1.0,\\,0,\\,0.2) ( 1.0 , 1.0 , 0.2 ) (1.0,\\,1.0,\\,0.2) Model learning rate 2 × 10 − 6 2\\times 10^{-6} 1.5 × 10 − 6 1.5\\times 10^{-6} Aligner learning rate 1 × 10 − 5 1\\times 10^{-5} 7.5 × 10 − 6 7.5\\times 10^{-6} We use Qwen3.6-27B (Qwen Team 2026b ) as the frozen examiner for ESR and ECTR. Each input produces eight rollouts with a temperature of 0.8, top- p p of 0.95, and top- k k of 50. The per-device batch size is 1, the gradient accumulation step is 8, and the generation batch size is 128. We set the KL coefficient to 0.06, weight decay to 0.1, gradient clipping to 0.5, and warmup ratio to 0.03 with a cosine learning-rate schedule whose minimum rate is 0.1 of the initial value. The maximum prompt and completion lengths are 14,336 and 4,096 tokens, respectively. Baselines We compare AudioMap with proprietary and open-source models under the evaluation protocols reported in Table 2 and 3. We organize the baselines by model access and task specialization. Proprietary models. The audio-only comparison includes GPT-4o Audio (Hurst et al. 2024 ) , Gemini-2.5-Flash (Comanici et al. 2025 ) , Gemini-2.5-Pro (Comanici et al. 2025 ) , Gemini-3.1-Pro (Google DeepMind 2026 ) , and Qwen3.5-Omni-Plus (Qwen Team 2026a ) . The audio-visual comparison uses the three Gemini models and Qwen3.5-Omni-Plus (Qwen Team 2026a ) . These API-based systems provide general-purpose reference points for dense captioning from audio and audio-visual inputs. Open-source generalist models. The audio-only comparison includes Qwen2-Audio-7B (Chu et al. 2024 ) , Qwen2.5-Omni at 3B and 7B scales (Xu et al. 2025a ) , Kimi-Audio-7B (Ding et al. 2025 ) , MiDashengLM-7B (Dinkel et al. 2025 ) , Step-Audio-2-mini-8B (Wu et al. 2025 ) , and Audio Flamingo 3-7B (Ghosh et al. 2026 ) . For audio-visual evaluation, we include both Qwen2.5-Omni scales and Qwen3-Omni-Instruct-30B-A3B (Xu et al. 2025b ) . Caption-specialized models. We compare with Omni-Captioner-7B (Ma et al. 2026b ) , AVoCaDO-7B (Chen et al. 2026b ) , and TimeChat-Captioner-7B (Yao et al. 2026 ) . Omni-Captioner targets detailed captioning, while AVoCaDO and TimeChat-Captioner are designed for audio-visual captioning. TimeChat-Captioner further emphasizes time-aware, structured descriptions. We also include the larger Qwen3-Omni-Captioner-30B-A3B (Xu et al. 2025b ) in the audio-only comparison. TAC (Kumar et al. 2026 ) is not included because neither its model weights nor its training data are publicly available. Experimental support, please view the build logs for errors. Generated by L A T E xml . Instructions for reporting errors We are continuing to improve HTML versions of papers, and your feedback helps enhance accessibility and mobile support. To report errors in the HTML that will help us improve conversion and rendering, choose any of the methods listed below: Click the \"Report Issue\" ( ) button, located in the page header. Tip: You can select the relevant text first, to include it in your report. Our team has already identified the following issues . We appreciate your time reviewing and reporting rendering errors we may not have found yet. Your efforts will help us improve the HTML versions for all readers, because disability should not be a barrier to accessing research. Thank you for your continued support in championing open access for all. Have a free development cycle? Help support accessibility at arXiv! Our collaborators at LaTeXML maintain a list of packages that need conversion , and welcome developer contributions . We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders , member institutions , , and all contributors. About &middot; Help &middot; Contact &middot; Subscribe &middot; Copyright &middot; Privacy &middot; Accessibility &middot; Operational Status (opens in new tab) Major funding support from \n", "numLines": 2, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 2}}}
